my dearest Zahra,

I've sat down to write this letter more times than I can count. Every time, I've stared at the screen, typed a few words, deleted them and started again. I didn't know how to fit three to four years of love into sentences. I didn't know how to say goodbye without saying goodbye, or how to hold on without holding you back. Today I've decided to stop waiting for the perfect words, because I don't think they exist. What I have instead are honest words that come from the deepest and truest part of me. I'm asking you, with all the gentleness in my heart, to read this slowly. Please read it more than once if you need to. Read it with your heart and not just your eyes, because every line here is something I've felt, prayed about, cried over and thought through for a long time before writing it down.

This letter is long because what we had was not small. I don't want to rush through it. I don't want to leave anything unsaid, or leave you wondering about anything later. I want you to know exactly where I stand, exactly how I feel, and exactly what I'm asking of you. I want to be fully honest about my love, my hope, my regret, my patience and my limits. You deserve that honesty, and so do I. After everything we've been through together, the least I can give you is the whole truth, laid out clearly.

Before anything else, I want to say the most important thing, so that whatever else you take from this letter, you take this first. I love you, Zahra. I love you in a way I didn't know I was capable of loving another person. It isn't a passing feeling or something that comes and goes with my mood. It isn't a habit that formed because we spent so much time together. It's deeper than that. It's rooted in who you are, in everything I've seen of you and in everything we've built. I love you with intention, patience and hope, and I want to build my whole life around that love. I want to marry you. I wanted to marry you yesterday, I want to marry you today, and if Allah wills, I'll still want to marry you tomorrow and every day after that.

I need you to understand that my love for you hasn't been shaken by what has happened between us. The distance and your decision haven't made me love you less. If anything, they've shown me how much I truly love you, because even in the pain, even in the confusion and even in the hurt, my heart still turns towards you. When something goes wrong in my day, I still want to tell you. When something good happens, you're still the first person I think of. When I picture my future, you're still the one standing next to me in it. That hasn't changed, and I don't believe it will.

I want you to know this love isn't only a feeling. It's a choice I make every day. Feelings can rise and fall like the sea, but a choice stays steady. Every morning I wake up and choose to love you. I choose to hope for us, to pray for you and to keep our dream alive. I'm not making that choice out of desperation or because I can't imagine anything else. I'm making it because I know what we had, I know what we could have, and I truly believe it's worth everything.

When I look back on our time together, I don't just see a relationship. I see a foundation, something solid that we built with our own hands, piece by piece, over years. I see the very beginning, when everything was new and uncertain and we were still learning each other. I remember how nervous I used to feel and how much I wanted you to like me. I remember slowly realising that this wasn't just anything, that you were someone special, someone I could see a real future with. I remember the moment I knew, deep down, that I wanted you to be my wife. Since that moment, nothing has changed my mind.

Over those years, I learned you in a way you only learn someone when you truly care. I know how your voice changes when you're tired, and how it softens when you're happy. I know the look on your face when you're trying to be strong but you're hurting inside, and how you try to hide it so others don't worry. I know what makes you laugh so hard you can't breathe, and I know what makes you go quiet. I know your fears, the ones you speak about and the ones you don't. I know your dreams, the big ones and the small ones. I know the things you've told almost no one, and I know the things you never had to say because I could already feel them. That kind of knowing doesn't happen overnight, Zahra. It takes years, patience and love, and we gave each other all three.

I think about all the conversations we had, the ones that went on for hours and somehow felt like minutes. We talked about everything and nothing. We talked about our families, our faith, our worries, our ambitions and our silly little thoughts. Sometimes we talked about deep things that made us think. Sometimes we just laughed until we forgot what we were laughing about. Those conversations were where I fell in love with your mind and your heart. They were where I learned how you see the world, and where I started to see the world a little differently myself.

I think about the hard days too, because a relationship isn't only made of beautiful moments. We had difficult times. We argued, misunderstood each other and hurt each other without meaning to. Even in those moments, we kept coming back. We learned how to argue and then find our way back to each other. We learned how to say sorry, how to forgive, and how to try again. We learned each other's silences and knew when to give space and when to come closer. That's what real love looks like to me. It's not the absence of difficulty. It's choosing each other again and again, even when it's hard.

I remember the times we held each other up. When you were going through something, I wanted to be the one beside you, carrying whatever I could so you didn't have to carry it alone. When I was struggling, you were there for me in ways I'll never forget. You made me feel seen and understood. You made me feel like I had someone in my corner no matter what. That's not something I take lightly. That's not something I'll ever forget.

We spoke about the future, Zahra, and not as a fantasy or a distant daydream, but as something real that we were walking towards together. I remember the plans and the little details. We talked about the kind of home we'd have, the kind of marriage we wanted, and the kind of parents we hoped to be one day. We talked about the values we wanted to live by and the faith we wanted at the centre of our home. We imagined a life that felt like ours, shaped by both of us and full of love, respect, laughter and barakah. Those conversations weren't empty words to me. They were promises I wanted to keep. They were the blueprint of the life I still want to build with you.

Those years weren't small. They weren't nothing. They weren't a phase or a chapter we can simply close and forget. They were years of effort, growth, sacrifice and love. We built them together, conversation by conversation, through hard days and beautiful ones. We invested our time, our hearts, our trust and our hopes. When I think about letting all of that go, it feels like watching a house we built with our own hands being knocked down before we ever got to live in it.

That kind of connection is rare. Some people search their whole lives and never find what we had. Some people never find someone who knows them the way we know each other, and who understands their heart without needing every word explained. I don't say this to pressure you. I say it because I believe it with everything in me, and because I don't want either of us to forget how precious it is. I don't want us to throw away the hard work we both put in. I don't want years of love, patience and growth to become a memory we only think about with sadness.

I know that sometimes, when things get hard, it's easy to focus only on the pain. It's easy to remember the arguments, the mistakes and the moments that hurt, and to forget all the good that came before and in between. I understand that, because I've felt it too. But I hope that when you think of us, you'll let yourself remember the whole picture. I hope you'll remember the laughter as well as the tears, the comfort as well as the conflict, and the peace as well as the pressure. I hope you'll remember that, for most of our time together, we were happy, we were good for each other, and we were building something beautiful.

Now I need to be honest with you, and with myself. I know I made mistakes. There were one or two things I did that I wish with all my heart I could undo. I won't make excuses for them. I won't brush them aside, minimise them, or pretend they didn't matter. I know they hurt you. I know they made you question things, and I know they played a part in where we are now. I'm sorry. I'm truly and deeply sorry. I'm not sorry because of what it cost me. I'm sorry because you deserved better from me in those moments, and I didn't give you what you deserved.

I've thought about those moments more than you could imagine. I've replayed them in my head late at night. I've asked myself why I acted the way I did and how I could have handled things differently. I've felt the weight of them in my chest. I've made dua asking Allah to forgive me and to help me become better. Through all of that reflection, I've come to understand something important: I can't change the past, no matter how much I wish I could, but I can choose who I become from here.

I'm choosing to grow. I'm choosing to learn from what I did wrong. I'm choosing to become a man who is more patient, more understanding and more thoughtful. I want to be more careful with your heart and more aware of how my words and actions affect the people I love. I want to become a man who is more worthy of the trust you gave me. I'm not saying this just to win you back with nice words. Words are easy, and I know that. I'm saying it because it's true, and because I want you to know that the man who loves you is also doing the hard work of becoming better, for you, for us, and for himself.

I want you to know that I don't see my mistakes as something to hide from. I see them as lessons, painful ones that I'll carry with me so I never repeat them. If Allah gives us another chance, I want you to see the difference in me. I want you to feel safer with me than you ever have before. I want you to never have to wonder whether I've truly changed. I want my actions to speak so clearly that you never have any doubt.

I also know that it wasn't only my mistakes that brought us here. There were things outside of us too, external factors and pressures that neither of us fully controlled. Life put weight on us that wasn't always fair. There were circumstances, opinions, expectations and situations that made everything harder. I understand that those things weighed on you. I understand that they may have made you feel uncertain or afraid, or made the path ahead look more difficult than it really is. I don't blame you for feeling the weight of them. I just wish we could have carried that weight together, the way we carried so much else.

I truly believe that external factors don't have to decide our future. Circumstances change. Pressures ease. Situations that feel impossible today can look completely different a year from now. What matters most is what's in our hearts and whether we're both willing to face whatever comes, side by side. I was willing then, and I'm still willing now. Whatever obstacles stood in our way, I would face every single one of them with you, if you'd let me.

You made the decision that you don't want to go forward and marry me. I want you to know that I've heard you. I've heard you clearly, and I haven't ignored or dismissed what you said. It hurts more than I can properly put into words. It feels like a part of me has been taken away. But I respect that it's your decision to make. You have every right to choose your own path, and I'll never try to take that away from you.

I'll never try to force you, guilt you, or pressure you into a life you don't freely choose. Love that's forced isn't love at all. A marriage built on pressure would never be the marriage I dreamed of with you. If you ever come back to me, I want it to be because your heart is completely sure. I want it to be because you've thought it through, prayed about it, and know without doubt that this is what you want. I don't want you to come back because you felt pushed, because you felt sorry for me, or because you felt obligated. I want you to come back only because you truly choose me.

But I also need to be truthful about where I stand, because honesty is the only thing I have left to offer right now. I didn't end this. I still want you. I still want to marry you. My answer has never changed, and it isn't changing now. You chose to step away. I'm choosing to stay hopeful. I need you to remember that, not as a way of blaming you, but simply as the truth of where things are. The door didn't close from my side. It's still open, and I'm still standing there.

So let me make my intentions as clear as I possibly can, so that you never have to guess or wonder.

I have hope. I'll wait for you, however long it takes. I mean that with every part of me. I'm not saying it lightly or in the heat of emotion. I've thought about it carefully and deeply. I've sat with it, prayed about it and asked myself whether I'm truly ready for what it means. My answer is yes. I know what I'm committing to. I know it might be a long time, and I know there are no guarantees. I'm choosing to wait anyway, because you're worth waiting for.

I won't be getting to know new people. I won't be putting myself out there. I won't be looking for someone else to fill the space you left. Nobody could fill it, and I don't want anyone to try. That space belongs to you. It has your name on it. It's shaped exactly like you, and nobody else would ever fit. I'm not going to pretend I'm moving on when my heart is still completely yours. I'm not going to waste someone else's time or hurt someone else by offering them a heart that already belongs to someone. My heart is where it has always been, with you.

I know some people might think I'm foolish for waiting. Some might tell me to move on, forget you and find someone new. I understand why they might say that, and I know they'd mean well. But they don't know what we had. They don't know what I feel. They haven't walked the road we walked together. I'd rather wait with hope for the person I truly love than rush into something empty just to avoid being alone. Waiting for you isn't a weakness. It's the strongest thing I know how to do.

I want to be clear that waiting doesn't mean I'll be sitting around feeling sorry for myself. It doesn't mean I'll stop living. It means my heart stays loyal while my life keeps moving forward. I'll keep working, growing and building, but I'll do all of it with you in my heart and with the hope that one day it will all be for us.

I'll keep praying for you. I'll pray for you in every salah. I'll raise my hands in dua for you in the quiet of the night, when the world is asleep and it's just me and Allah. I'll pray for your happiness and for your health. I'll pray for your peace of mind, for your heart to be calm, and for your worries to be lifted. I'll pray that Allah guides you, protects you and never leaves you without His mercy. I'll pray that He gives you clarity in your decisions and contentment in your heart.

And I'll pray, with every bit of honesty in me, that what's best for you is me. I'll pray that Allah softens your heart towards us again. I'll pray that He removes whatever obstacles stand between us, whether they come from my mistakes, from outside pressures, or from fears inside your heart. I'll pray that one day you come back and choose us. I'll ask Allah to write us for each other, and if it's already written, to bring it to us at the right time and in the best way.

I also know that Allah knows what we don't. I trust that He is the best of planners. So alongside my dua for us, I'll also pray for the strength to accept whatever He decrees. I'll ask Him to give me patience, sabr, the kind of patience that is beautiful and doesn't complain. I'll ask Him to keep my heart steady and my faith strong while I wait. I'm placing my hope in you, but I'm placing my trust in Allah, and I know He won't let that trust go to waste.

There's comfort for me in knowing that even if we aren't speaking, I can still do something for you every day. I can still pray for you. I can still ask Allah to protect you. Even in the silence, I'll be with you in that way. Every dua I make for you is a quiet way of saying I still love you, I still care, and I'm still here.

While I wait, I won't be standing still. I'll keep building the dream I've always had for us. I'll keep working on myself, my future, and the life I want to offer you. I'll focus on becoming more stable, more grounded and more prepared, in my work, my finances, my character and my faith. I want to be the kind of man who can give you everything you deserve. I want to be ready in every sense of the word.

I want that, if the day ever comes that you return, you return to a man who has grown. I want you to find someone steadier, stronger and more ready than ever to be your husband. I want you to see straight away that the time apart wasn't wasted, and that I used it to become better for us. I won't let our dream die just because we're apart. I'll carry it for both of us until you're ready to carry it with me again.

I think about the home I want to build for us. I think about making it a place of peace, a place where you feel completely safe, loved and respected. I think about filling it with faith, laughter and warmth. I think about the life we could have, the mornings and evenings, the ordinary days that would feel extraordinary because we'd be sharing them. I'm not letting go of that picture. I'm working towards it every day, brick by brick, so that if you ever say yes, it's ready.

I know a dream can feel fragile when only one person is holding it. But I'm strong enough to hold it for now. I'll keep it safe. I'll keep it alive. I'll protect it from the doubts and the fears and the voices telling me to give up. When you're ready, I'll hand it back to you, and we can hold it together, just like we always planned.

Now I need to explain something important, and I need you to understand it fully, because it comes from a place of love and self-respect, not anger.

From here on, I believe it's best that we stay in no contact. I mean no communication at all, and I really do mean none. That means no messages, no calls, no voice notes, no checking in and no small talk.

I know that sounds hard. Maybe it even sounds cold or harsh. Please believe me when I say it isn't coming from a cold place. It's coming from a place of real and deep pain. I'm already partly broken inside, Zahra. I'm trying to hold myself together, keep my faith strong and keep my hope alive. Every message, every "how are you", every small catch-up would reopen a wound that I'm trying so hard to live with. It would give me a little hope and then take it away again, over and over. I can't live like that. I can't be half in and half out of your life. It would break me slowly, piece by piece, until there was nothing left.

I need you to understand how contact would feel on my side. If you messaged me just to talk, my heart would leap. For a moment, I'd think maybe you were coming back. Then I'd realise it was just a conversation, just a moment of missing me, and nothing had changed. Then I'd fall again, harder than before. Each time, it would take me days or weeks to get back to where I was. I can't keep going through that cycle. I don't think my heart could survive it.

So I'm asking you, with all the gentleness I have, please don't message me unless you're ready to move forward. I mean ready to marry me and ready to build our life together properly, with commitment, intention and certainty.

That means if you're feeling down, please don't message me. If you're upset, please don't message me. If you're sad, please don't message me. If you're lonely, please don't message me. Even if you're missing me, and I know there may be times when you do, please don't message me.

I know that's difficult. I know there may be moments when you want to reach out because you're hurting or because you miss what we had. There may be nights when you think of me and want to hear my voice. There may be days when something reminds you of us and you feel the urge to message me. I'm asking you to hold back in those moments. Missing me isn't enough. Feeling low isn't enough. Wanting comfort isn't enough. A catch-up isn't enough. I don't want those things, because they aren't fair to my heart. They would bring me close enough to feel you again, then leave me standing alone once more.

Please don't think I'm saying this because I don't care how you feel. I do care, deeply. If you're sad, it hurts me to think of you sad. If you're struggling, I wish I could be there. But I can't be your comfort and then be left again. I can't be the person you turn to when you need support but not the person you choose to build a life with. I love you too much to accept a smaller role in your life. Please lean on your family, your friends, your faith, and the people around you who can support you in those moments. Please let me be the one you come to only when you come to stay.

The only message I want from you, the only one I'm waiting for, is the one where you tell me you're ready. I'm waiting for the message that says you've made your choice, you're choosing me, and you want to move forward towards marriage. If that message ever comes, I'll be here. I'll answer with an open heart. Until then, please let the silence be.

I want you to know that this silence doesn't mean I've stopped loving you. It's the opposite. The silence is how I protect the love I have for you. It's how I protect what's left of my heart, so it's still whole enough to give to you if you ever come back. It's how I keep my hope pure and stop it being worn down by confusion and mixed signals. This silence isn't a wall between us. It's a space where both of us can think clearly, heal, and decide what we truly want.

I also believe the silence will help you. Without my messages and my presence pulling at you, you'll be able to think honestly about what you want. You'll be able to feel whether you truly miss me, or whether you're at peace without me. You'll be able to make a decision with a clear heart and mind. If you come back after that, I'll know it's real. If you don't, then at least we'll both know the truth.

I also need to talk about the other path, the one I pray never happens. I don't want to avoid it or pretend it isn't possible, because that wouldn't be honest. You deserve honesty, and so do I.

If you decide to move on, then by all means, do. You're free. I won't stand in your way. I won't chase you or beg you. I won't make your life difficult or try to interfere. I won't speak badly of you to anyone. I want you to live the life that you choose, even if that life doesn't include me. Your happiness matters to me, even when it hurts me.

But I need you to know the truth: the day I find out that you've found someone else and you're marrying him will be one of the most painful days of my life. It will break my heart in a way I don't think I'll ever fully recover from. I'll grieve the life we could have had. I'll grieve the dream I carried for so long. I'll grieve you. I'm telling you this not to make you feel guilty or to put any weight on your shoulders. I'm telling you because it's the honest truth of how much you mean to me. I don't want to hide that from you. I hope with my whole heart that day never comes. I hope instead that the day comes when you choose me.

I'll trust Allah even then. If that day comes, I'll ask Him to give me the strength to accept it, heal and find peace. I won't pretend it will be easy, because it won't. But I'll lean on my faith and the people who love me. I'll trust that Allah doesn't place a burden on a soul greater than it can bear. Even so, I pray that it's a burden He never asks me to carry.

There's one more thing I need to make completely clear, and I need you to take it seriously, because I mean every single word.

If you do find someone else, and you get to know him, go on dates with him, share things with him, open up to him and let him into your life, and he's looking out for you and caring for you, and it turns out he's the right person for you, then congratulations. I mean that sincerely. I'll pray that you're happy, that he treats you well, and that you find the peace you deserve.

But if he turns out not to be the right one, please understand this: I'm not an option you can come back to. I'm not a backup plan. I'm not a second choice. I'm not somewhere safe to land when something else doesn't work out. I'm not a fallback or a consolation.

If I ever find out that you went to someone else first, giving him your time, your heart, your trust and your attention, and then came back to me only because it didn't work out with him, I won't take you back. I'm saying that with love, but I'm saying it firmly, and I need you to believe me. I won't change my mind on this. No apology or explanation will change it. Once you choose that path, it closes this one permanently.

It's not to punish you. It's not anger or pride. It's because I know my worth, and I know what I'm offering you. I'm offering you all of me, my whole heart, my loyalty, my patience, my faith and my entire future. Someone who offers that much deserves to be chosen first. They don't deserve to be settled for after something else falls apart. They don't deserve to be the leftover, the second best or the safe option. I'm not asking for much. I'm only asking to be chosen wholeheartedly and without hesitation.

It's either me or nothing. It's 100% me, marrying me and building our life together, or it's nothing at all. There's no in-between. I won't be on the sidelines of your life, watching from a distance and waiting to be noticed. I won't wait in the shadows while you look for something better, then accept being what's left over. I love you too much to accept that, and I respect myself too much to allow it.

So please be careful with how you move forward. Think deeply before you decide. Pray about it and pray istikhara. Sit with your heart and ask yourself honestly what you want. If you choose someone else, that's your right, but understand that choosing him means letting me go completely. You can't explore another path and keep this one waiting behind you. If I'm gone, I'm totally gone. I'm serious about that, Zahra. I'm not saying it to scare you, test you or manipulate you. I'm saying it because it's the only way I can protect my heart, and because I believe you deserve to know exactly where I stand before you make any decision.

I'd rather you hear this from me now, clearly and honestly, than find out later and feel I never warned you. I'm laying it all out so that whatever you decide, you decide with your eyes open. I'm not hiding anything. I'm not leaving anything vague. This is the truth of my heart and the line I won't cross.

You might wonder why I'd wait for someone who has chosen to step away. You might wonder why I'd put my life on hold for someone who hasn't promised me anything. The answer is simple. I believe in us. I believe in what we built. I believe that love like ours is worth more than the mistakes, the pressures and the circumstances that got in the way. I believe Allah brought us together for a reason. I'm not ready to give up on that reason.

I've seen who you are, Zahra. I've seen you when life is gentle and when it's hard. I've seen your kindness, the way you care for others even when you're tired. I've seen your strength, the way you keep going even when things feel heavy. I've seen your softness, the gentle side of you that not everyone gets to see. I've seen your stubbornness too, and I love that as well. I love every part of you. I don't just love the best version of you. I love all of you: the parts you show the world and the parts you keep hidden, your good days and your bad days, your confidence and your doubts.

I've known your heart, and it's a heart worth waiting for. It's a heart full of love, faith and goodness. It's a heart that deserves to be cherished, protected and honoured. I want to be the one who does that. I want to be the one who makes you feel safe for the rest of your life, the one you come home to, and the one who stands by you through everything.

When I imagine my future, you're in it. I can see us building a home together and filling it with warmth, laughter, faith and love. I can see us praying together and starting our days with gratitude. I can see us supporting each other's dreams and celebrating each other's achievements. I can see us comforting each other through the hard times and growing closer because of them. I can see us growing older together and laughing about the time we nearly let this slip away. I can see us raising a family on everything we learned from each other, teaching our children what love, patience and faith look like. That isn't a fantasy to me. It's a dream I've carried for years, and I'm not ready to let it go.

I want you to know I'm not holding on to an idea of you. I'm holding on to you, the real you, with all your beauty and all your flaws. I know you're not perfect, and I'm certainly not either. But I believe we're right for each other. I believe that together we can become better than we could ever be apart.

I want you to know what my life will look like while I wait, so you never have to wonder or worry.

I'll be working on myself every day. I'll be facing the things I did wrong and making sure I never repeat them. I'll be reflecting on how I can be a better partner, a better listener, and a better man. I'll be building my future through my work, my stability, my faith and my character. I'll be strengthening my relationship with Allah, because I know that a man who's close to Allah is a better husband, a better friend, and a better person.

I'll be praying for you, for us, and for the wisdom to accept whatever is written for us. My prayers will be my way of staying connected to you while we're apart. Every sujood will carry your name. Every dua will carry our dream.

I won't be looking at anyone else. I won't be filling my time with distractions or pretending to move on. I won't be seeking attention or comfort from anyone else. My heart is where it has always been. I won't be bitter towards you or speak badly of you, because I could never resent someone I love this much. I'll remember the good: your smile, your voice, the way you made ordinary days feel special, and the way you made me feel at home.

I'll be patient, even on the nights when patience feels impossible. There will be moments when I miss you so much it physically hurts. There will be moments when I want to message you, hear your voice, and just know you're okay. I'll hold back, because I've asked you to hold back too, and because I know the silence is what we both need right now. Instead, I'll put that longing into my prayers. I'll turn my missing you into dua. I'll trust that Allah hears every one of them.

I'll also take care of myself. I'll lean on my family and my friends. I'll keep myself busy with things that are good for me. I won't let this pain swallow me, because I want to be whole and ready if you ever come back. I owe that to you, and I owe it to myself.

I can't control what you do, and I won't try. But I hope you'll take the time you need to think, and really think, about what you want and what you're walking away from. I hope you'll look past the hurt, the mistakes and the outside pressures, and remember what we had at our best. I hope you'll ask yourself honestly whether the reasons for ending things are bigger than the reasons for staying.

I hope you'll remember the conversations that went on for hours and felt like minutes. I hope you'll remember the way we looked after each other and the times we made each other feel safe. I hope you'll remember the plans we made together and the future we dreamed of. I hope you'll remember how it felt to be completely understood by someone. I hope you'll remember that I've always wanted you, not for a season, but for a lifetime.

I hope you'll pray about it. I hope you'll ask Allah for guidance and listen for His answer. I hope you'll pray istikhara with a sincere heart, open to whatever He shows you. If He guides you back to me, I'll be here. If He guides you elsewhere, I'll accept it, even though it will break me.

Take care of yourself, Zahra. Please look after your health and your heart. Eat well, rest well, and don't carry everything alone. Surround yourself with people who love you and want what's best for you. Lean on your faith, because it will carry you when nothing else can. Be gentle with yourself. Whatever you decide, I want you to be okay. Even in this pain, your wellbeing matters to me more than I can say.

I don't want you to carry guilt because of this letter. I'm not writing it to make you feel bad or to weigh you down. I'm writing it so you know the truth, so there are no misunderstandings, and so that whatever happens next, we both know exactly where we stand. You don't owe me anything except honesty. If you ever come back, come back honestly. If you never do, I'll understand that as your answer.

I want to bring everything together in one place, so there's no confusion and nothing left unsaid.

I love you. I want to marry you. That hasn't changed and it won't.

I'm sorry for the mistakes I made. I own them completely, and I'm working every day to become a better man.

I understand that external factors made things harder, and I don't blame you for feeling their weight. I'd still face all of them with you.

You made the decision to end things, not me. I respect your choice, even though it breaks my heart. I'll never pressure or force you.

I'll wait for you, however long it takes. I won't be seeing anyone else, getting to know anyone new, or looking for anyone to replace you. I'll keep praying for you, keep making dua for us, and keep building the dream I have for our life together.

